To connect a mobile request to its backend trace, instrument both the mobile network client and backend service. Allow tracing only for approved **first-party hosts**: the API domains your team controls. The services must preserve trace headers and retain the relevant sampled trace. These headers identify related requests; they do not authenticate or encrypt them. [Connect RUM and Traces](https://docs.datadoghq.com/tracing/other_telemetry/rum/).

## 2. Obtain access and application configuration

Complete these steps before adding the SDK:

1. Request the **Datadog Observability Entitlement** through your Enterprise identity/access portal.
2. After approval, open Datadog through Enterprise SSO.
3. The authorized app owner locates the existing RUM application or creates the approved application if none exists. Use the actual app type: Android, iOS, or React Native.
4. Record application ID, client token, approved Datadog site, environment, service/release naming, consent handling, and approved session/trace sampling.
5. Confirm who owns dashboards, alert definitions, backend instrumentation, and release symbol uploads.

Open the RUM application setup area in your Datadog tenant. Navigation labels can vary. Reuse an existing approved application rather than creating a duplicate. [Android application setup](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/android/setup/), [iOS application setup](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/ios/setup/), [React Native application setup](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/react_native/setup/).

**Attach screenshot — application type, approved site, and setup status, with tokens and sensitive identifiers hidden.**

### Obtain the configuration values

Ask the application/observability owner for the following values. Use your approved configuration system; this table is a handoff reference, not an SDK configuration file.

| Configuration | What you need |
| :--- | :--- |
| Application | Existing RUM application ID and platform. |
| Intake | Client token and matching Datadog site. |
| Release identity | Environment, service, app version, and build identifier. |
| Collection | Consent behavior and RUM/trace sampling rates. |
| Network tracing | Explicit first-party host allowlist and backend owner. |
| Operations | Dashboard/alert owner and release-symbol upload owner. |

**Expected result:** you can open the correct RUM application and identify the configuration for your non-production build. If access or a value is missing, contact its owner before configuring the SDK.

### Use the correct credential

| Value | Where it belongs / purpose |
| :--- | :--- |
| RUM application ID | Approved mobile configuration; identifies the RUM application. |
| Client token | Mobile ingestion configuration. It is designed for client applications; do not substitute a Datadog API key. |
| Datadog API key used for symbol uploads | Protected CI/tooling binding; never package it inside the app or commit it. |
| Site, environment, service, version/build | Actual approved values associated with the application/release. SDK enum names and site hostnames are not necessarily interchangeable strings. |

A client token is not a general management credential or permission to transmit confidential data. [Official client-token/key distinction](https://docs.datadoghq.com/account_management/api-app-keys/).

