## 4. Approve the data before enabling collection

| Area | Required Enterprise handling |
| :--- | :--- |
| Credentials/headers | Do not attach Authorization, cookies, API keys, or raw authentication tokens. |
| URLs/error messages | Remove sensitive query/path values and payload fragments using the approved field/mapper controls. |
| Custom attributes | Allow only approved diagnostic fields; do not dump request/response objects or arbitrary Redux state. |
| User identity | Use an approved pseudonymous identifier. Do not send raw email addresses or assume hashing an email makes it anonymous. |
| Consent | Use the app's approved consent state and withdrawal behavior; do not hardcode `granted` simply to get events into the dashboard. |
| Session Replay | Requires its own approval, supported SDK/configuration, and tested privacy settings; input masking does not sanitize every log/resource attribute. |

Sampling controls how much telemetry is collected; it does not remove sensitive fields. Configure and test the pinned SDK's event filters and replay privacy controls separately. [Session Replay privacy](https://docs.datadoghq.com/session_replay/privacy_options/), [Android data handling](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/android/data_collected/), and [iOS consent configuration](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/ios/advanced_configuration/).

SDK configuration alone does not establish legal compliance. Enterprise privacy requirements remain part of application design and approved data handling.

### Safe event-design preview — My Chicken App

This is a **documentation example**, not a Datadog SDK method or an Enterprise event schema:

```json
{
  "event_name": "chicken_order.confirmed",
  "attributes": {
    "item_count": 2,
    "outcome": "confirmed"
  }
}
```

Emit a confirmed event only after an actual valid receipt, not when the submit button is tapped. Do not attach customer names, addresses, payment details, order payloads, or raw order IDs to make the event easier to find. The app owner approves any additional correlation identifier and its privacy/retention treatment.

## 5. Understand the performance targets

Use these Enterprise targets when agreeing measurements with the observability owner. Define the scope and measurement window before creating alerts:

| Target | Measurement definition |
| :--- | :--- |
| Time to Initial Display (TTID) ≤ 800 ms | Time until the first application frame is displayed during launch. Confirm platform/SDK support and launch type. |
| Time to Full Display (TTFD) ≤ 2.0 s | Time until the application reports its initial content ready. Define the ready condition and platform instrumentation. |
| Slow frames ≤ 5% | Confirm platform metric, numerator/denominator, refresh-rate handling, and aggregation. |
| Sessions with frozen frames ≤ 0.1% | Define the percentage of measured sessions containing a frozen frame. Do not substitute a percentage of frames. |
| Crash-free sessions ≥ 99.85% | Define application/environment/version, time window, sample/denominator policy, and alert ownership. |

Datadog defines frozen frames as taking **longer than 700 ms** to render. Keep this duration separate from the target percentage of sessions affected. Rendering measurements differ across platforms. [Datadog Mobile Vitals](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/mobile_vitals/).

TTID/TTFD collection is version/platform-specific. Datadog's current Android launch monitoring defines these during application launch; it is not automatic proof of identical metrics for every screen on every SDK. [Android launch monitoring](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/android/application_launch_monitoring/).

Do not create a monitor from a target alone. The owner must supply metric/query, scope, aggregation, time window, evaluation behavior, notification destination, and sampling treatment.

## 6. Verify one controlled journey

After implementing the integration page:

1. Run an approved non-production build with known environment/service/version values and the appropriate consent state.
2. In My Chicken App, open the ordering screen, place an order using synthetic data, and wait for its confirmation.
3. Open the RUM application and filter by that environment, app version, and test time. Find the ordering view, submission action, and confirmed event configured by the integration.
4. Inspect an eligible correlated request only if mobile and backend tracing are both configured; missing correlation is an investigation, not permission to broaden host capture.
5. Inspect the event fields for sensitive data. For crashes, verify a controlled test report against matching symbols using the approved non-production procedure.

**Expected result:** the configured events appear under the intended application, environment, and version, without sensitive fields. If they are absent, check consent, site/token/application configuration, sampling, and network delivery before adding more events.

SDK batching, network conditions, consent, sampling, processing, and symbol availability affect visibility. A successful initialization log is not proof of ingestion, complete capture, readable crash stacks, or correctly configured alerts. [Android setup and ingestion behavior](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/android/setup/).

**Attach screenshot — sanitized RUM journey with the intended environment/version, and its eligible trace/error association.**

## 7. Continue in order

| Page | Developer work |
| :--- | :--- |
| [**Datadog Mobile Integration**](./datadog-mobile-integration.mdx) | Platform dependency/configuration snippets, initialization, views/actions/errors, network instrumentation, privacy/consent, and release-symbol upload integration. |
| [**Monitoring & Troubleshooting**](./monitoring-and-troubleshooting.mdx) | Verify ingestion, investigate performance/errors, diagnose missing symbols/traces, and validate owner-defined dashboards/alerts. |

**Developer:** owns application instrumentation and safe diagnostic attributes. **Platform/observability owner:** supplies access, site/application configuration, approved bindings, dashboards, and monitors. **Backend owner:** supplies compatible APM instrumentation. **Privacy/security owner:** confirms the permitted collection/identity/replay policy.

CI/CD retains protected symbol-upload credentials and release identity. Testing verifies expected behavior before delivery; observability supplies runtime evidence and does not replace tests or release gates.

