If the project does not use Bundler, use its documented CocoaPods command. Commit the selected JS lockfile and `Podfile.lock`. Rebuild native apps; a Metro reload does not install native modules. [React Native SDK installation](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/react_native/setup/?tab=rum).

### B. Configure a single provider

Suggested files:

```text
src/observability/
├── datadog.ts
└── order-telemetry.ts
App.tsx                         # Existing root; preserve providers/navigation
```

Create `src/observability/datadog.ts`:

```typescript
import {
  DatadogProviderConfiguration,
  TrackingConsent,
} from '@datadog/mobile-react-native';

type Options = ConstructorParameters<typeof DatadogProviderConfiguration>[3];

type MobileConfiguration = {
  clientToken: string;
  environment: string;
  applicationId: string;
  service: string;
  site: NonNullable<NonNullable<Options>['site']>;
  sessionSampleRate: number;
  consent: TrackingConsent;
};

export function createDatadogConfiguration(input: MobileConfiguration) {
  const required = [input.clientToken, input.environment, input.applicationId, input.service];
  if (required.some(value => !value.trim())) {
    throw new Error('Missing observability configuration');
  }
  if (!Number.isFinite(input.sessionSampleRate) ||
      input.sessionSampleRate < 0 || input.sessionSampleRate > 100) {
    throw new Error('Invalid observability sampling rate');
  }

  return new DatadogProviderConfiguration(
    input.clientToken,
    input.environment,
    input.consent,
    {
      site: input.site,
      service: input.service,
      rumConfiguration: {
        applicationId: input.applicationId,
        sessionSampleRate: input.sessionSampleRate,
        trackInteractions: false,
        trackResources: false,
        trackErrors: false,
        nativeCrashReportEnabled: false,
      },
    },
  );
}
```

This starts with manual views/actions and the listed automatic capture features disabled. It uses the 3.x constructor shape; use the matching migration guide when upgrading an existing 2.x installation. [Provider configuration](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/react_native/setup/?tab=rum), [3.x migration and constructor](https://github.com/DataDog/dd-sdk-reactnative/blob/develop/MIGRATION.md).

In `App.tsx`, wrap the existing application tree with **one** provider:

```tsx
// Merge into your existing root; do not replace its other providers.
import { DatadogProvider } from '@datadog/mobile-react-native';

// configuration is the stable result of createDatadogConfiguration(...),
// created once from approved app configuration before mounting this tree.
<DatadogProvider configuration={configuration}>
  <ExistingApplicationTree />
</DatadogProvider>
```

`configuration` and `ExistingApplicationTree` represent your existing app bindings, not additional SDK exports. Do not assume `process.env` is automatically populated in a mobile release. Preserve the Redux Toolkit store and other root providers. Update consent through the pinned SDK's documented consent API; do not remount/reinitialize the provider as a consent mechanism. [Consent and advanced configuration](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/react_native/advanced_configuration/).

### C. Add explicit view and action calls

Create `src/observability/order-telemetry.ts`:

```typescript
import { DdRum, RumActionType } from '@datadog/mobile-react-native';

// Telemetry delivery must not break ordering or trigger an order retry.
function send(operation: () => Promise<unknown>): void {
  void Promise.resolve().then(operation).catch(() => {
    // No raw error, payload, or credential logging here.
  });
}

export function startOrderView(viewKey: string): void {
  send(() => DdRum.startView(viewKey, 'Chicken Order', {}, Date.now()));
}

export function stopOrderView(viewKey: string): void {
  send(() => DdRum.stopView(viewKey, {}, Date.now()));
}

export function recordConfirmedOrder(itemCount: number): void {
  if (!Number.isSafeInteger(itemCount) || itemCount <= 0) return;
  send(() => DdRum.addAction(
    RumActionType.CUSTOM,
    'chicken_order.confirmed',
    { item_count: itemCount, outcome: 'confirmed' },
    Date.now(),
  ));
}
```

Call `startOrderView` on **screen focus** with a stable, unique key for that screen instance; call `stopOrderView` on blur using the same key. Mount/unmount alone is insufficient when navigation keeps screens mounted. Record confirmation from the existing successful-order handler, not a render effect. [Manual RUM instrumentation](https://docs.datadoghq.com/client_sdks/advanced_configuration/).

For an existing React Navigation screen, merge this focus binding into the screen component:

```tsx
import { useCallback } from 'react';
import { useFocusEffect, useRoute } from '@react-navigation/native';
import { startOrderView, stopOrderView } from './observability/order-telemetry';

// Inside the existing order screen; adjust the import path above.
const route = useRoute();
useFocusEffect(useCallback(() => {
  startOrderView(route.key);
  return () => stopOrderView(route.key);
}, [route.key]));
```

The route key identifies the mounted navigation entry; it must not contain personal data. Emit confirmation only from a verified success handler while the intended view is active. Do not pull the UI back to the order screen or retry a purchase merely to attach telemetry.

For React Navigation, you can instead add the compatible `@datadog/mobile-react-navigation` integration and track navigation from `NavigationContainer.onReady`. Do not also manually start/stop those views. Route names/parameters must not expose user data. [Navigation integration](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/react_native/integrated_libraries/).

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

## 3. Add network tracing and error capture deliberately

After the basic view/action journey works, enable the signals required by your app:

| Platform | Integration work | Official instructions |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Android | Add compatible `dd-sdk-android-okhttp` and tracing modules. Enable tracing, configure approved hosts, and attach the interceptor to the actual application OkHttp client. An unused client captures nothing. | [Android RUM setup](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/android/setup/), [tracing integration](https://docs.datadoghq.com/tracing/trace_collection/custom_instrumentation/client-side/android/dd-api/). |
| iOS | Add the tracing product where needed; configure RUM URLSession tracking, approved hosts, and URLSession instrumentation for the delegate/session actually used by the app. | [iOS advanced configuration](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/ios/advanced_configuration/). |
| React Native | Enable `trackResources`, `trackErrors`, and native crash reporting only after reviewing captured data. Configure `firstPartyHosts` and trace sampling with the matching SDK API. Native networking outside the supported JS instrumentation needs its own integration. | [React Native configuration](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/react_native/advanced_configuration/). |

Do not send raw exception messages or response objects as custom attributes. Use stable error categories and approved field filtering. Session Replay requires separate configuration and privacy validation; it is not enabled by these examples. [Replay privacy controls](https://docs.datadoghq.com/session_replay/privacy_options/).

**Expected result:** the intended request appears as a RUM resource. A trace link appears only when trace propagation, backend instrumentation, and sampling support that request. [Connect RUM and APM](https://docs.datadoghq.com/tracing/other_telemetry/rum/).

## 4. Keep symbols with the exact release

| Build | Retain and upload |
| :--- | :--- |
| Android | R8/ProGuard mapping for the actual obfuscated variant; native symbols if required by the enabled crash integration. |
| iOS | dSYMs from the exact device archive. |
| React Native | JS bundle/source maps for each platform, plus the applicable native Android mappings and iOS dSYMs. |

Use protected **Datadog API key** credentials for uploads, not the mobile client token. Pin the approved upload tool/plugin and preserve release association. [Android symbol upload](https://docs.datadoghq.com/error_tracking/frontend/mobile/android/), [iOS symbol upload](https://docs.datadoghq.com/error_tracking/frontend/mobile/ios/), [React Native source maps](https://docs.datadoghq.com/error_tracking/frontend/mobile/reactnative/).

For iOS, the following is a **Bitrise workflow step fragment** to place after the archive and its dSYMs exist. It assumes an approved `datadog-ci` executable is already on the macOS runner's PATH:

```yaml
- script@1:
    title: Upload matching iOS dSYMs to Datadog
    inputs:
      - content: |-
          #!/usr/bin/env bash
          set -euo pipefail
          : "${DATADOG_API_KEY:?Bind through protected CI credentials}"
          : "${DATADOG_SITE:?Set the approved CLI site hostname}"
          : "${BITRISE_DSYM_PATH:?Archive must produce the matching dSYMs}"
          command -v datadog-ci >/dev/null
          test -e "$BITRISE_DSYM_PATH"
          datadog-ci dsyms upload "$BITRISE_DSYM_PATH"
```

`DATADOG_SITE` here is the CLI hostname, such as `datadoghq.com`, not the SDK enum `US1`. Keep shell tracing disabled and never print credentials. Upload completion still needs a readable crash-stack verification. [Datadog dSYM upload command](https://docs.datadoghq.com/error_tracking/frontend/mobile/ios/).

## 5. Run and inspect one journey

1. Build and launch the app through its existing non-production run command/Xcode scheme.
2. Apply the appropriate test consent state and use the approved non-production sampling configuration.
3. Open the chicken-order screen. Place an order with synthetic data and wait for a valid confirmation.
4. Open the correct RUM application. Filter by environment, app version, and the test time.
5. Find the ordering view (**Chicken Order** in the manual examples) and **chicken_order.confirmed**. Inspect `item_count` and `outcome`; confirm no confidential fields were attached.
6. After network/error instrumentation is enabled, inspect one eligible request and an approved controlled error/crash. For native crashes, relaunch the app so the SDK can upload its report.

**Attach screenshot — sanitized ordering view and confirmed action, with environment/version filters visible.**

| Observation | Next action |
| :--- | :--- |
| SDK initialization succeeds but no view appears | Confirm view tracking is actually called, consent allows upload, and the session is sampled. |
| Events appear under the wrong app/site | Check the client token, application ID, and site as a matched configuration. |
| Confirmed action repeats | Check for render-effect instrumentation or duplicate success handlers. Do not resubmit an order to repair telemetry. |
| Resource appears without a backend trace | Check approved hosts, trace propagation, backend instrumentation, and sampling. |
| Stack frames remain unreadable | Check the exact release's source maps/mappings/dSYMs and their upload association. |

